we must ensure that the root is never removed, and required symbols nodes are never removed.\n\nAdditionally, the \"utility\" field might be for ordering? The description mentions \"registered_candidate_order\" and \"stable_ties\". It says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So ordering of candidates: first by depth descending (deeper first), then by node ID ascending? Probably alphabetical/numeric. And we need to return removal order (list of IDs in the order they were removed). So we iterate through nodes in that order, and for each candidate node (removable true, not root, not required symbol, and all required symbols remain represented if we remove its subtree, and no children retained (but since we process deeper first, children will have been processed before parent; if any child was retained, then parent cannot remove, but if all children were removed, then parent can be removed if it satisfies other conditions). Actually we need to check: when we consider a node, its children may have been removed already. So we should check that all children of this node are already removed (i.e., none are retained). If any child is retained, then we cannot remove this node because it would have retained children. So we can only remove a node if its entire subtree is already removed (except itself). That is consistent: we remove leaves first. So algorithm:\n\n1. Build map id->node.\n2. Build parent-child relationships.\n3. Identify all nodes that are required (symbol in required_symbols) and root (parent null). Mark them as cannot be removed.\n4. Initialize a set of removed IDs (empty) and a list removal_order.\n5. Determine candidate order: sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending. For each node in that order:\n   - If node id is in cannot_remove set (root or required), skip.\n   - If not removable, skip.\n   - If node has any child that is not in removed set (i.e., still retained), then skip.\n   - Compute set of symbols in subtree of this node (including node itself). To compute, we need to know which nodes are still retained in subtree? But we are removing the entire subtree, which includes all descendants, whether already removed or not? Actually if descendants are already removed, they are not in the tree anymore. But the subtree of a node includes all its descendants that are still present? But if some descendants are already removed, the subtree is not contiguous. The concept of removing a node: it removes the node and any retained descendants. But if descendants are already removed, then removing the node just removes itself and doesn't affect others. However, the check \"all required symbols remain represented\" should consider that after removal, the set of symbols from retained nodes (outside this subtree) still covers required_symbols. So we need to compute the union of symbols from all currently retained nodes, and see if after removing this node and its retained descendants (i.e., everything in its subtree that is still retained), the required symbols are still all present. That is equivalent to: for each required symbol, there must be at least one node with that symbol that is not in this node's subtree and not already removed. So we need to know for each required symbol, are there any occurrences outside the subtree? The easiest: precompute for each node the set of symbols in its retained subtree (including itself). Then after processing some removals, we maintain a global set of symbols from retained nodes. For a candidate node, we need to check if the global set of symbols minus the symbols in its retained subtree still contains all required symbols. But careful: the retained subtree may change as we remove nodes. Since we process in order, we can compute dynamically.\n\nWe can maintain:\n- retained_nodes set (initially all nodes).\n- For each node, we can compute the set of symbols in its current retained subtree (including itself). But as we remove nodes, we need to update. Since we remove leaves first, we can compute on the fly: when we consider a node, we can compute the union of its symbol plus the symbols from its children that are still retained (since children are either removed or retained). This can be done by walking the subtree each time? Could be inefficient but nodes are small. Alternatively, we can compute for each node the total set of symbols in its original subtree (all descendants), and then subtract those that have been removed. But since we only remove whole subtrees (when a node is removed, its entire retained subtree is removed), we can just check: if we remove this node, we remove all nodes in its retained subtree (which we can compute as current retained descendants). So we need to know for each required symbol, if there is at least one node with that symbol outside this subtree. That is: the global retained set before removal contains the symbol; after removal, we need that there is at least one node with that symbol not in the removed subtree.\n\nSimpler: For each required symbol, we can keep a count of how many nodes with that symbol are currently retained. When we consider removing a node, we need to ensure that for each required symbol, the count after removal (i.e., current count minus number of occurrences of that symbol in the node's retained subtree) is > 0. So we need to know, for the candidate node, how many nodes of each required symbol are in its retained subtree. Since nodes are small, we can compute by DFS from the node, collecting symbols only from nodes that are currently retained (i.e., not removed). Because when processing deeper first, the subtree is unchanged (no nodes removed from it yet, except those already removed as part of lower subtrees). Actually when we process leaf first, we remove leaves. Their parent's subtree loses those leaves. So we need to recalculate the subtree symbols each time. That's okay.\n\nImplementation approach:\n- Build nodes list into dict id->node_data\n- Build adjacency children list for each node.\n- Initialize set removed = set()\n- Initialize list removal_order = []\n- Determine cannot_remove = {root_id} ∪ {node.id for node if node.symbol in required_symbols}\n- Create list of candidate nodes sorted by (-depth, id) (descending depth, ascending id)\n- For each node in sorted list:\n   - if node.id in cannot_remove or not node.removable: continue\n   - Check if node has any child that is not in removed (i.e., child.id not in removed). If yes: skip (because it would leave retained children). (Note: children that are already removed are fine.)\n   - Compute subtree_symbol_counts: for each required symbol, count how many nodes in the retained subtree of this node (including node) have that symbol. We can do a DFS from node, traversing only children that are not removed (i.e., children that are still retained). Since the node itself may be required? But it's not in cannot_remove (we already skipped if symbol in required_symbols). So node's symbol is not required but could be something else. But we still need to count any required symbols in its subtree.\n   - For each required symbol, compute current global count (we need to maintain global counts). Let's maintain global_required_counts dict from symbol to count of nodes with that symbol not removed. Initially compute from all nodes. Then when we remove a subtree, we decrement counts.\n   - Check: for each required symbol, global_required_counts[symbol] - subtree_counts.get(symbol,0) > 0. If all >0, then we can remove.\n   - If can remove: add node.id to removal_order, add to removed set. Update global_required_counts by subtracting subtree_counts. Also, we need to update the children? Actually when we remove a node, its entire retained subtree is removed. But we have already processed children earlier (deeper first) and those children that were removable and satisfied conditions would have been removed already. So the retained subtree of this node may include some children that were not removed (because they were not removable or were required). But if they were not removed, then they are still retained, which would violate the check \"node with retained children\" that we already did. So any child that is not removed is either not removable or required. But we already checked that node has no retained children (i.e., all children are either removed or not present). Actually we checked: \"if node has any child that is not in removed set, skip.\" That means any child that is still retained (not removed) will cause skip. So only nodes with all children already removed (or no children) will be considered. So the subtree below this node consists only of already-removed nodes (and this node itself). So the count of required symbols in subtree is just from this node itself. So we don't need DFS; we just check if this node's symbol is required? But the node is not required (since we skipped required). So subtree_counts are zero. That makes sense: because all children are already removed, they don't contribute. So the check simplifies: after removing this node, we lose only its own symbol (which is not required). So required symbols remain. However, there is a nuance: a child may be not removable but also not required? But that child would be retained, which would cause skip. So indeed, only leaves or nodes whose entire subtree below has been removed are candidates. So we can skip the DFS and just check that the node's own symbol is not required (already ensured by cannot_remove). So the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" is automatically satisfied because we are only removing nodes that don't carry required symbols and their subtrees are already gone. But wait: what if a child is a required symbol node? Then that child is in cannot_remove, so it was never removed, and it is retained. Then the parent would have a retained child, so we skip parent. So parent never gets removed. So indeed, required symbols act as anchors that prevent their ancestors from being removed.\n\nThus the algorithm is simpler: process nodes in descending depth order. For each node that is removable, not root, not required, and has all children already removed (i.e., no retained children), then remove it. Add to removal order.\n\nBut is there any case where a node with no children (leaf) is removable, not required, not root, and we remove it? Yes. Then its parent may later become candidate when all its children are removed.\n\nWe need to return retained_node_ids sorted (alphabetically? numeric? node IDs are strings like \"root\", \"n1\", etc. Should be sorted lexicographically). And removed_node_ids in order of removal.\n\nLet's test with the provided examples? We can simulate mentally for first case:\n\nnodes:\nroot: depth0, not removable, required? root in required_symbols list? required_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"] so root is required, never remove.\nn1: depth1, symbol s1, removable false, required (s1) => never remove.\nn2: depth1, symbol s2, removable true, not required (s2 not in required). children: n4.\nn3: depth1, symbol s0, removable false, not required? s0 not in required, but removable false => not candidate.\nn4: depth2, symbol s1, removable true, but symbol s1 is required so cannot remove (even if removable true). So n4 stays.\nn5: depth2, symbol s2, removable false, not required.\nn6: depth2, symbol s0, removable true, not required.\n\nNow process by depth descending: depth2 nodes: n4 (required -> skip), n5 (removable false -> skip), n6 (removable true, not required, not root, check children: none, so can remove). Remove n6. Add to removal_order: [\"n6\"]. Update removed set.\nNow depth1 nodes: order: n1, n2, n3 (by id? Actually depth1: n1, n2, n3. n1: required -> skip; n2: removable true, not required, check children: child n4 is still retained (not removed), so cannot remove because has retained child. n3: removable false -> skip.\nNow depth0: root: skip.\nSo only n6 removed. retained nodes: all others. Sorted retained IDs: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? Actually root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Sorted lexicographically: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? But \"n1\" < \"n2\" etc., \"root\" < \"n1\"? \"r\" < \"n\"? ASCII: 'r' > 'n', so \"root\" is after \"n5\"? Actually 'r' (114) > 'n' (110), so \"n1\" < \"n2\" < \"n3\" < \"n4\" < \"n5\" < \"root\". So retained_node_ids = [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"].\n\nNow check second case: similar, n6 has removable true, not required, no children. So same result: only n6 removed. Third case: n6 removable true, not required, no children. Same.\n\nSo all three fixtures would give same pattern: removed_node_ids: [\"n6\"], retained: all others sorted.\n\nBut the description mentions \"utility\" field; perhaps it influences ordering? The instruction says \"registered_candidate_order\" and \"stable_ties\". Could be that utility is used for ordering instead of depth? But the text says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" No mention of utility for ordering. However, \"utility\" might be used for \"interestingness_preserved\"? Or maybe for deciding which nodes to remove when multiple candidates at same depth? But tie break is node ID. So utility not used in ordering.\n\nThus implementation is straightforward.\n\nEdge cases: if no candidate can be removed, return empty removal list.\n\nWe must raise ValueError for infeasible or structurally invalid case. Possibly if required symbols are missing from the tree initially? Or if root is not present? We should check that the payload has required fields: nodes list, required_symbols list. Check each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also check that tree is acyclic? Not specified. We'll assume valid. If any required symbol is not present in the tree (i.e., no node with that symbol), then it's infeasible? The spec says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So we should check that every required symbol has at least one node (not necessarily not removable). If not, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso ensure there is exactly one root (parent null). If multiple roots or no root, invalid.\n\nNow write solve(case) that returns dict with removed_node_ids list and retained_node_ids list.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Validate case structure: ensure 'nodes' and 'required_symbols' exist.\n2. Build dict nodes_by_id = {node['id']: node for node in case['nodes']}\n3. Check for required symbols: for sym in case['required_symbols']: ensure any node with symbol == sym exists, else ValueError.\n4. Find root nodes: nodes where parent is None. Exactly one, else ValueError.\n5. Build children map: for each node, add to parent's children list if parent is not None and parent exists.\n6. Mark cannot_remove: root id and all node ids where node['symbol'] in required_symbols (set).\n7. Initialize removed = set(), removal_order = []\n8. Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending (as string).\n9. For each node in order:\n   - if node['id'] in cannot_remove or not node['removable']: continue\n   - check children: for each child in children[node['id']]: if child not in removed: skip this node (cannot remove)\n   - (If passes) add to removal_order, add to removed set.\n10. After loop, retained_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in case['nodes'] if n['id'] not in removed])\n11. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removal_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nNote: The output schema requires \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\" both arrays of strings. No other keys.\n\nAlso, we must not mutate case.\n\nNow produce the implementation JSON.\n\nWe need to output JSON with key \"implementation\" and value a string containing Python source code. The source code should define `solve(case)`.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines and quotes properly in JSON.\n\nWrite the Python code.\n\nPotential issues: \n- node IDs may not be in sorted order by default, but we sort explicitly.\n- Depth may be integer; we can sort by depth and then id.\n\nWe must use only standard library.\n\nReturn JSON-serializable object.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":5333,"total_tokens":7505,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4596},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}